= Introduction

Definitions:

*Invetory*: 
The amount of material goods held by an organization to:
+ support production
+ support activities
+ sale or customer service  

*Inventory management*: 
Maintain the *optimum number* or amount of each inventroy item
Uninterupted production, sales and / or customer-service levels at the *minimum cost*
Inventory is the largest item in the current asset category

Two forces influencing inventory:
- Supply (Supply chain efficiency)
  - bulk purchasing (econmies of scale)
  - production continuity
  - Optimize transprt and logitics
- Demand (Meet customer Demand)
  - Buffer against uncertainty
  - Seasonal Demand
  - Market speculation

- *Too much*
  - Shortage
  - Reputation damage
  - Production downtime
  - longer lead times
- *Too little*
  - obsolete or dead stock
  - Tied up capital / holding cost
  - Storage space
- *Just right*
  - Service levels
  - Hedge against variation in supply and demand
  - Smooth production

Roles of inventory
- *Cycle* stock:
  - Meet demand during each cycle (time interval between two consecutive replenishments / production runs)
- *Safety* stock (uncertainty)
  - Hedge against demand and supply variablility; ensure service levels
- *Pipeline* stock
  - Due to replenishment lead time
- *Decoupling* stock
  - Buffer between successive stages om a production process
- *Anticipation* stock (trend & seasonality)
  - Seasonal demand; seasonal production; speculation
  - Build inventory for high demand periods in periods of low demand (leaving production capacity unchanged)

- Multiple layers of business entities
- Multiple decision makers
- Multiple objectives

E.g.
- Fridge (food for a household)
- Replenishment policies
  - Some daily, some weekly, some monthly, etc.

What factors affect inventory management:
- Demand
  - Stable
  - Time varying (cyclical)
- Supply lead time:
  - Deterministic or stochastic
  - Load dependent
- Capacity
  - Ample or limited
- Review
  - Continuous or periodic
- Excess demand
  - Backordering or lost sales
  - Item substitution
- Item & customer classes
  - Single or multiple items
  - Single or multiple client classes
- Cost structure 
  - Unit cost, set-up cost, holding cost, shortage cost, penalties
- Objectives
  - Service level target (unknown shortage cost)
  - Minimize cost (known shortage cost)

